cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "frankwolfe-active-set-lookup"
abstract: >-
  An answer to FrankWolfe.jl issue #244, open since 2021: does anything beat
  the linear scan find_atom uses to decide whether the LMO's vertex is already
  in the active set? Two answers, both exact. Wherever the caller is a
  Frank-Wolfe step it already holds the gradient's inner product with the new
  vertex and with the active set's best atom, and the first being strictly
  smaller proves the vertex absent in one comparison, with no data structure at
  all; every call the measured BPCG runs made was decided that way, and the
  step rule guarantees it in exact arithmetic. For a caller that has only the
  atom, a folded structural hash with exact confirmation beats the scan at
  every measured active-set size. Every number is measured on real runs before
  anything is proposed upstream.
type: software
